LEAD = {
    "headline": "CREATOR ADVERTISING SCORES 99 OUT OF 100 ON THE NUMBER THAT SETS BUDGETS, AND 151 ON THE ONE THAT DOES NOT.  THAT GAP IS WHY THE WORK KEEPS GETTING CUT WHILE IT IS WORKING",
    "deck": "Meta published a 54-page paper on creator measurement on Wednesday, and the number holding it up is not Meta's.  It belongs to the IPA, and it has been sitting in public for a year.  Read it once and the whole pattern of brands trying creators, liking the results and quietly trimming the line makes sense.",
    "stamps": [
        ("PPC LAND · 11 OCT", "https://ppc.land/meta-says-partnership-ads-cut-incremental-purchase-costs-45-in-emea-tests/"),
        ("IPA · 8 OCT 2025", "https://ipa.co.uk/news/influencer-marketing"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "PPC Land's Luis Rijo published a close reading of Meta's new creator measurement paper yesterday evening.  The paper itself went up on 7 October, runs to 54 pages, and the finding it is built on comes from the IPA, the British advertising industry's trade body, which keeps a cross-industry database of <mark>220 campaigns from 144 brands</mark> across 36 sectors and 28 markets, covering more than 133 million pounds of influencer spend.",
        "Here is that finding.  Measured on short-term return, influencer marketing indexes <mark>99</mark>.  An index of 100 is the average across all channels, so 99 is dead average, fractionally ahead of linear television's 97 and well ahead of paid social's 86.  Measured on long-term return, the same channel indexes <mark>151</mark>, and its long-term multiplier of <mark>3.35</mark> is the highest of any channel the IPA measured, above linear television's 3.27.  And they do it from a very small base.  Influencers account for 4.5 percent of short-term sales, against linear television's 32 percent, and 6.2 percent of long-term sales.",
        "Now the mechanism, which is almost embarrassingly simple.  Budgets are set on the short-term number.  The quarterly review, the campaign post-mortem, the sheet the finance director actually reads: all of it measures return inside the campaign window.  On that number creator work is unremarkable, so it is the first line trimmed when money is tight.  The number on which it is best in class takes two or three years of modelling to produce, which means almost nobody has it when the decision is made.  Meta's own diagnosis of this is blunt, and correct: the gap is not belief or budget, it is infrastructure, meaning how creators are planned, briefed, bought and measured.",
        "Meta then sells you its fix, and the headline figure is a good one.  Across 20 conversion lift studies in Europe, the Middle East and Africa run between 1 January and 30 June 2025, advertisers who made at least 30 percent of a test cell's creative assets partnership ads — the format where the advert runs from the creator's own account rather than the brand's — recorded a <mark>45 percent lower median cost per incremental purchase</mark>, meaning the cost of a sale the advertising can be shown to have caused, and a 35 percent higher median incremental return.  A separate look at 10 advertisers across 684 ad sets in October 2025 found 63 percent of the people partnership ads reached had seen none of that brand's ordinary advertising in the same ad set over the same period.",
        "Three things about that before you repeat it.  The 45 percent figure carries 92 percent confidence, below the 95 percent threshold the industry conventionally treats as significant, and the paper does not mention the shortfall.  The 30 percent threshold is a share of creative assets inside a test, not a share of spend, while other figures in the same paper are shares of spend, and page 24 of Meta's own document restates the threshold as over 30 percent rather than at least 30.  And every one of these is a median across 20 studies, which is the middle result rather than the result you should expect.",
        "None of which makes the IPA gap less real.  That one was published a year ago, by a body with nothing to sell, and the industry read it and did nothing.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("99", "creator short-term return index, against an all-channel average of 100"),
        ("151", "the same channel measured on long-term return"),
        ("45%", "lower median cost per caused sale in Meta's own 20-study test"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Meta sells partnership ads, so every performance figure here except the IPA data is the platform's own analysis of its own product, measured in its own tools.  The 63 percent new-reach study describes its advertisers as based in Europe, the Middle East and Africa while roughly 43 percent of the spend was delivered in the United States.  The IPA's headline indices rest on 59 campaigns for the short-term figure and 18 for the long-term one, not the full 220.  We could not find a public Meta address for the paper, so its figures reach you through PPC Land; the report title Meta cites for the IPA data does not appear anywhere on the IPA's own site; and one figure PPC Land relays, a long-term multiplier of 1.98 for paid social, is not stated in the IPA's published material.",
    "so_what": "Creator video is judged on the metric it is worst at and paid for out of the budget line that gets cut first.  That is not a measurement problem, it is a timing problem: the evidence that justifies the spend arrives two or three years after the decision to spend.  Until somebody holds a creator budget across three planning cycles without flinching, every brand will keep rediscovering this finding and cutting the same line.",
    "do_this": "Commission long-term modelling on your last three creator campaigns this week, so a number exists before the next budget round rather than after it.  Separately, count what share of your live creative assets currently run from a creator's own account rather than your brand handle, and write a target share into the next brief as a figure somebody has to hit.",
}

